<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="integrations-jumpcloud"
      updated="August 18, 2026"
      evidence="Verified against a live JumpCloud tenant on August 18, 2026."
      toc={[
        {"sso", "Single sign-on"},
        {"scim", "Directory sync"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="integrations-jumpcloud">
        <:dek>
          Connect JumpCloud to emisar: one custom application carries OIDC sign-in and directory
          provisioning together.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>A <strong class="text-zinc-300">JumpCloud administrator</strong>.</:item>
        <:item>An emisar <strong class="text-zinc-300">owner or admin</strong>.</:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso">Single sign-on</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Connecting an identity provider is a paid feature. <.plan_note tier={:team} />
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>emisar</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Start the connection.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open
              <a
                href={~p"/app/sso/new"}
                class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
              >Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</a>
              and choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">JumpCloud</strong>. Leave the page open: the
              callback URL on it is what JumpCloud asks for next, and JumpCloud's values come back here
              at the end.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-emisar-add-provider.webp"
              title="emisar · Add provider"
              caption="Picking the provider here tailors the rest of the form to it."
              alt="emisar's Add provider form with the Provider type select outlined in green and set to JumpCloud."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>JumpCloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Open SSO Applications.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In the JumpCloud Admin Portal go to
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Access → SSO Applications</strong>
              and press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Add New Application</strong>.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-add-app.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · SSO Applications"
              caption="Every application JumpCloud signs people into is listed here. Provisioning shows against the ones that also push a directory."
              alt="The JumpCloud Admin Portal's SSO Applications page listing two configured applications, DEMO - RSA and emisar, the latter tagged Provisioning under Supported Functionality, with the Add New Application button at the top left outlined in green."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>JumpCloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Choose Custom Application.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              emisar is not in JumpCloud's catalog, so scroll past the featured tiles to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Custom Application</strong>, press its <strong class="text-zinc-200">Select</strong>, then <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next</strong>.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-custom-app.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Select Application"
              caption="The Custom Application tile sits last, after the catalog's featured integrations."
              alt="JumpCloud's Create New Application Integration wizard on step 1, Select Application, showing featured tiles for Google Workspace, AWS IAM Identity Center, Slack, Atlassian Cloud, Personio, CrowdStrike and Salesforce, with the Custom Application tile in the bottom-right corner outlined in green."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>JumpCloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Enable SSO, and pick OIDC.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Manage Single Sign-On (SSO)</strong>. That reveals a
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Select One</strong>
              pair beneath it — choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Configure SSO with OIDC</strong>.
              It defaults to SAML, and emisar's connection is OIDC, so this is the one click on the
              page that is easy to miss and hard to undo later.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4">
              If you also want directory sync, tick
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Export users to this app (Provisioning)</strong>
              on this same step — JumpCloud carries both on one application. Leave
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Import users from this app</strong>
              off; that direction pulls into JumpCloud.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-options.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Select Options"
              caption="SSO with OIDC and Export users, together on one application. Import users stays off — it points the wrong way."
              alt="JumpCloud's Select Options step with Manage Single Sign-On (SSO) checked and outlined in green, its Select One radio pair showing Configure SSO with OIDC selected rather than the default SAML; Export users to this app (Provisioning) is also checked, Import users from this app and Add a bookmark are unchecked, and the Next button is enabled."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            5
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>JumpCloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Name it.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Display Label</strong>
              is what your people see in the JumpCloud User Portal —
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar</code>
              is the obvious choice. Everything else on this step is cosmetic.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-general-info.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Enter General Info"
              caption="Only Display Label is required."
              alt="JumpCloud's Enter General Info step with the Display Label field filled in with emisar and focused, an empty optional Description, a Color Indicator picker set to blue, and Show this application in User Portal checked."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            6
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>JumpCloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Check the summary, then configure.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              The review step lists what you just chose under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled Features</strong>. It should read
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">SSO with OIDC</strong>
              — and <strong class="text-zinc-200">User Export - Provisioning</strong>
              if you ticked it. If it says SAML, go <strong class="text-zinc-200">Back</strong>
              and change the radio; emisar cannot use a SAML connector. Then press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Configure Application</strong>.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-review.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Review"
              caption="Enabled Features is the check that the OIDC radio actually took."
              alt="JumpCloud's Review step for an application about to be added, whose Application Summary panel shows Display Label emisar and Enabled Features listing both SSO with OIDC and User Export - Provisioning, outlined in green, beside a Configure Application button."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            7
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>JumpCloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Set the redirect URI, then activate.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              On the application's <strong class="text-zinc-200">SSO</strong>
              tab, put emisar's callback in <strong class="text-zinc-200">Redirect URIs</strong>:
            </p>
            <.docs_code
              phx-no-format
              label="Redirect URI"
              copy_text="https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback"
            >https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback</.docs_code>
            <p class="mt-4">
              Leave <strong class="text-zinc-200">Authorization Code</strong>
              as the grant type. Either <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client Secret POST</strong>
              or <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client Secret Basic</strong>
              works — emisar accepts both. Set <strong class="text-zinc-200">Login URL</strong>
              to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">https://emisar.dev</code>, then press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Activate</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-oidc-config.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · OIDC Single Sign-On Configuration"
              caption="Redirect URIs takes emisar's callback verbatim. Activate sits at the bottom right."
              alt="The SSO tab of a JumpCloud OpenID Connect application, with badges reading Single Sign-On Active, Client Secret Valid and Provisioning Inactive, and tabs for General Info, SSO, Provisioning, User Groups and Users. Under Configuration Settings the Authorization Code grant type is checked, the Redirect URIs label is outlined in green above a field containing https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback, Client Authentication Type is set to Client Secret POST, and an Activate button sits at the bottom right."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            8
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>emisar</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Paste the two values it reveals.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Activating shows the <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client ID</strong>
              and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client Secret</strong>. They are the only two
              values you carry back. On the connection you opened in step 1, the issuer is already
              filled in and locked — it is the same for every JumpCloud org — so those two fields
              are all there is to fill. Tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled</strong>, save, and sign in.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-emisar-credentials.webp"
              title="emisar · Add provider"
              caption="Nothing to look up: JumpCloud's issuer is fixed, so emisar fills and locks it."
              alt="emisar's OIDC connection fields with the Issuer URL locked at https://oauth.id.jumpcloud.com/ behind a padlock, and Client ID and Client secret each outlined in green below it."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="The rest of the connection form">
        <p>
          This guide covers only what JumpCloud needs. The other settings on the connection —
          who gets provisioned and at what role, what runner access they start with, whether this
          provider satisfies your 2FA requirement — are the same for every provider and are
          explained in <.link navigate={~p"/docs/sso"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Single sign-on</.link>.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="scim">Directory sync</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        If you ticked
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Export users to this app (Provisioning)</strong>
        on step 3, this same application pushes your directory — there is no second connector to
        create.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You need the connection's <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM base URL</strong>
        and <strong class="text-zinc-200">bearer token</strong>
        —
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim#enable"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >enabling directory sync</.link>
        shows where both come from. <.plan_note tier={:enterprise} />
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>JumpCloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Point its Provisioning tab at emisar.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open the saved app's <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>
              tab (JumpCloud's own docs also call it <em>Identity Management</em>). The SCIM
              version is fixed at 2.0; set
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Authentication method</strong>
              to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Bearer token</strong>, then fill
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Base URL</strong>
              with emisar's SCIM base URL and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Token Key</strong>
              with the bearer. <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test User Email</strong>
              must be an address that doesn't exist in emisar yet — JumpCloud provisions and then
              deletes it as a live check, so an existing member would fail activation.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-scim-connection.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Provisioning"
              caption="Test Connection is a live check: JumpCloud calls emisar before it will activate."
              alt="The Provisioning tab of a JumpCloud OIDC application with Authentication method set to Bearer token, and Base URL and Token Key outlined in green."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <.docs_step_venue>JumpCloud</.docs_step_venue>
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Test Connection, then Activate.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test Connection</strong>, then <strong class="text-zinc-200">Activate</strong>. Activation is not a formality: it
              creates, renames and deletes a probe group, and provisions and removes the test user,
              against your live emisar. Do <strong class="text-zinc-200">not</strong>
              press Save instead — JumpCloud discards the configuration and you start over.
              The probe group may omit <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>;
              emisar returns an id that JumpCloud uses for the rename and delete.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-scim-activate.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Provisioning"
              caption="The button reads Test Connection until the check passes, then becomes Activate."
              alt="The Provisioning tab of a JumpCloud OIDC application after a successful connection test. Authentication method is Bearer token, Base URL and Token Key are outlined in green and filled, and Test User Email holds an address that does not exist in emisar. The footer now offers Cancel, Undo Changes and Activate, where it read Test Connection before the check ran."
            />
            <p class="mt-4">
              Then bind the people you want synced: the app's
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">User Groups</strong>
              tab decides who is in scope. Members appear under
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Synced users</strong>
              on the emisar connection, and their JumpCloud groups become mappable to roles below.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h3 id="one-person-one-member">When sign-in and the directory disagree</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar binds a synced member on the
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
        the directory sends, falling back to
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">userName</code>
        when it sends none — and it binds a sign-in on the
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
        claim. When those two values differ, emisar cannot tell that the person signing in is the
        one the directory synced, so it holds their sign-in for an admin instead of guessing.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You do not have to make them match. A sign-in the directory has not claimed lands in
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pending access requests</strong>
        under <a
          href={~p"/app/team"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team</a>, with the identifier it presented. Approving it once binds that sign-in to
        the member the directory already synced — one person, one member, both identifiers on the
        same record — and every later sign-in and directory push finds them. That is one approval
        per person, the first time they sign in.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Watch the first one to be sure it is the same person: the request shows the email JumpCloud
        asserted, and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Synced users</strong>
        on the connection under
        <a
          href={~p"/app/sso"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team → Single sign-on</a>
        shows who the directory sent. If those disagree, do not approve — that is
        a misconfigured connector, not a mismatched identifier.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-8 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next:</strong>
        map directory groups to emisar roles so access follows your IdP — <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim#role-mapping"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >role mapping</.link>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
